% model_router — pick the model for a run so nobody in the room has to

# Module: model_router

## Intent

CoPrompt reaches many vendors through one TokenRouter key. TokenRouter has no
server-side "auto" model, so the choice has to be made here — and it should not
be made by the people in the room. A team directing an agent together should not
have to stop and argue about model names.

This module picks. Given the catalog the router advertises and a little context
about the run, it returns one model id and a short reason the room can read.

Pure decision logic over a catalog. No network, no I/O, no clock, no global
state — the catalog is passed in.

## Interface

```python
MESSAGES_ENDPOINTS = ("anthropic", "anthropic-compatible")

def eligible(catalog: list[dict]) -> list[dict]:
    ...

def choose(
    catalog: list[dict],
    *,
    difficulty: str = "standard",
    prefer: str | None = None,
) -> dict:
    ...
```

- `catalog` is TokenRouter's `/v1/models` payload as a list of
  `{"id": str, "supported_endpoint_types": [str], "tags": str}`.
- `eligible` filters the catalog down to models this app can actually call.
- `difficulty` is one of `"cheap"`, `"standard"`, `"hard"`.
- `prefer` is an optional exact model id. When it is present AND eligible, it
  wins outright — an explicit human choice always beats the router.
- `choose` returns `{"model": str, "reason": str, "tier": str}`.

## MUST NOT

- MUST NOT return a model whose `supported_endpoint_types` contains none of
  `MESSAGES_ENDPOINTS`. This app speaks the Messages API; anything else fails at
  request time, which is a worse experience than not offering it.
- MUST NOT return a model whose `tags` mention `Image`, `Video`, `Audio`, or
  `Embedding`, even if its endpoint type would otherwise qualify. A run
  generates text and calls tools.
- MUST NOT return a `prefer` value that is not in the eligible set. Fall back to
  the normal choice and say so in `reason`.
- MUST NOT be non-deterministic. The same catalog and the same arguments MUST
  return the same model every time, so two teammates never see different
  answers. Break every tie by model id ascending.
- MUST NOT mutate `catalog` or any dict inside it.
- MUST NOT raise when the catalog is empty or nothing is eligible; raise
  `NoEligibleModel` instead, which is a distinct exception the caller can catch.

## MUST

- MUST prefer, within a tier, a model that is already known to be fast: an id
  containing `fast`, `haiku`, `flash`, or `lite` is the fast end of its family.
- MUST rank families by capability, strongest first: `opus`, then `sonnet`,
  then everything else, then `haiku`.
- MUST map difficulty to a tier:
  - `"cheap"` — the fastest eligible model, preferring `haiku`/`fast`/`lite`
  - `"standard"` — a mid model, preferring `sonnet`, falling back to the fast
    end of a stronger family before reaching for a full-size one
  - `"hard"` — the strongest eligible model, preferring full-size `opus`
- MUST include, in `reason`, both the tier and why that model won, in one short
  sentence a non-engineer can read.
- MUST return the highest-numbered version when two ids differ only by version
  (`claude-opus-4.7` vs `claude-opus-4.8` → prefer `4.8`).
- MUST raise `ValueError` for an unknown `difficulty`.

## Examples

Given a catalog containing `anthropic/claude-opus-5`,
`anthropic/claude-opus-5-fast`, `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5`,
`anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5`, `google/gemini-3.5-flash` (openai endpoint only),
and `openai/gpt-5-image-mini` (image):

```python
choose(cat, difficulty="hard")["model"]      # -> "anthropic/claude-opus-5"
choose(cat, difficulty="cheap")["model"]     # -> "anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5"
choose(cat, difficulty="standard")["model"]  # -> "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5"

# Not eligible: wrong endpoint, and an image model.
[m["id"] for m in eligible(cat)]
# -> never contains "google/gemini-3.5-flash" or "openai/gpt-5-image-mini"

# An explicit choice wins.
choose(cat, prefer="anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5", difficulty="hard")["model"]
# -> "anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5"

# An explicit choice that is not eligible does not.
r = choose(cat, prefer="openai/gpt-5-image-mini", difficulty="hard")
r["model"]                    # -> "anthropic/claude-opus-5"
"not available" in r["reason"]  # -> True

choose([], difficulty="hard")  # raises NoEligibleModel
```

## Done when

- Every example above returns exactly the stated result.
- Two calls with identical arguments always agree.
- The module has no imports beyond the standard library.
